{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ללכת בדרכיו", :translit "la'lekhet bid'rakhav", :gloss "to walk in His ways"} {:he "ללמוד תורה", :translit "lilmod torah", :gloss "to learn Torah"} {:he "וליראה אותו", :translit "ul'yir'ah oto", :gloss "and to fear Him"} {:he "למה", :translit "lamah", :gloss "for what"} {:he "כי ה' אלהיך מביאך אל ארץ טובה", :translit "ki Hashem elohekha mevi'kha el eretz tovah", :gloss "for YHWH your God brings you into a good land"} {:he "ולא תהיה כפוי טובה", :translit "velo tihyeh kafui tovah", :gloss "and you shall not be an ingrate"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ללכת בדרכיו", :b "ללמוד תורה"}], :frame {:text "The Sifrei on this verse explains that 'to walk in His ways' means to emulate God's attributes — just as He is merciful, you be merciful (Sifrei Devarim 49; cf. Shabbat 133b). The phrase frames Torah study as the practical means of that imitation. The concern about ingratitude after receiving the good land echoes the broader warning of the passage: when you eat and are satisfied, remember that it is God who gave you the land (Deuteronomy 8:10-18).", :sources ["Sifrei Devarim 49" "Shabbat 133b" "Deuteronomy 8:10"]}}], :arithmetic [{:a-value 722, :b-value 721, :lens :standard, :equal? false, :kind :gematria, :b "ללמוד תורה", :by :selah, :colel? true, :a "ללכת בדרכיו"}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:35:14.978982802Z", :model "glm-5.2"}